{"id":"7ae4832d-a272-435c-8ce4-0c5520e9b7f4","object":"chat.completion","created":1784837414,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case[\\\"payload\\\"]\\n    nodes = payload[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n    node_map = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: [] for n in nodes}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n[\\\"parent\\\"]\\n        if pid is None:\\n            root_id = n[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        else:\\n            children[pid].append(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removal_order = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if n[\\\"symbol\\\"] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if not n[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n        # collect retained subtree\\n        subtree = set()\\n        stack = [nid]\\n        while stack:\\n            cur = stack.pop()\\n            if cur in retained:\\n                subtree.add(cur)\\n                for ch in children.get(cur, []):\\n                    if ch in retained:\\n                        stack.append(ch)\\n        # check required symbols\\n        hypothetical = retained - subtree\\n        if all(any(node_map[nn][\\\"symbol\\\"] == sym for nn in hypothetical) for sym in required_symbols):\\n            retained = hypothetical\\n            removal_order.append(nid)\\n    sorted_retained = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"retained_ids\\\": sorted_retained, \\\"removal_order\\\": removal_order}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` that performs grammar-guided node reduction. The task is to reduce a parse tree (nodes with parent, depth, symbol, removable, utility) given required symbols. The algorithm: Perses-style reduction: prioritize deeper nodes, then by depth tie-breaker? The description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So traversal order: sort nodes by depth descending, then by ID ascending (or stable). For each node (tentatively), if removable, and not root, and symbol not in required_symbols, and all required symbols remain represented in the tree after removal (i.e., there is at least one node with each required symbol that is not removed or that would be retained after removal? Actually we need to check if removing that node would cause any required symbol to be absent from the tree. But nodes that are removable may have children. If we remove a node, we also remove its descendants (since it's a tree). The rule: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That means after removal, the required symbols must still be present in the tree (among retained nodes). Also \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Wait: \"a node with retained children\"? Possibly means: if a node has children that are retained (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove the parent because that would orphan children? But in tree reduction, removing a node typically removes its entire subtree. However Perses-style reduction often replaces a node with its children (or deletes if it's a leaf). But the description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That suggests we cannot remove a node if it has children that are not removed? Actually \"retained children\" means children that are kept; if we remove the parent, the children are also removed (since they are descendants). So \"retained children\" might be misinterpreted. Let's read carefully: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" I think it means: if a node has children that are themselves retained (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove that node because that would remove the children? But if we remove the node, all children and descendants are removed, so the children would not be retained. So maybe it means: do not remove a node that has children that are not scheduled for removal? But that's circular. Alternatively, it might mean: we cannot remove a node that has children that are themselves candidates for removal? No. Let's think of Perses: It reduces by trying to delete a node and replacing it with its children (if grammar allows) or deleting it entirely. But here we have a simple tree with symbols and no grammar rules. The objective is \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement\" but we have no grammar rules. The bounded objective: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" But the interestingness oracle is not provided; we assume it's based on required symbols? The case payload does not include an interestingness function. So presumably the \"interestingness\" is that the tree after reduction must still contain all required symbols. That is the only constraint given. Also \"grammar-valid\" might mean that the resulting tree is still a valid parse tree? But without grammar, we cannot check that. So likely the algorithm is simply: remove removable nodes (that are not root, not required symbol) one by one in order of depth descending, as long as after removal the set of symbols of retained nodes still covers all required symbols. And we need to return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order.\n\nBut the \"Removal order\" is the order in which nodes were actually removed (i.e., the sequence of IDs removed). We need to simulate the reduction.\n\nKey points:\n- Nodes have parent (string), depth, removable (bool), utility (float, not used?).\n- required_symbols list.\n- Root is always present and not removable.\n- \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" This likely means: if a node has children that are still in the tree (i.e., not yet removed), then removing that node would remove those children, so we cannot remove it unless we first remove all its children. But the algorithm processes nodes in depth order (deeper first). So deeper nodes are considered before their ancestors. So if a node is removable, we check if removing it would cause loss of required symbols. If not, we remove it. Then later when we consider its parent, the parent may have lost some children (because they were removed). So the parent's children are not retained. So the condition \"node with retained children\" would be false if all its children have been removed already. So we should not remove a node that still has children that are still present (i.e., not removed). But that is automatic because we process deeper first, so children are considered before parent. However if a child is removable but we choose not to remove it (because removal would violate required symbols), then the parent still has that child retained. In that case, we should not remove the parent because it would remove that child. So the rule is: do not remove a node if any of its children are still in the tree (i.e., not removed). So we need to keep track of which nodes are removed. Or simply, we only consider removal of a node if all its children have been removed (or it has no children). But the order of processing ensures that children are considered first. So when we consider a node, its children may or may not have been removed. If any child remains, we skip removing this node.\n\nThus algorithm:\n1. Build a map from id to node dict.\n2. Build children lists from parent references.\n3. Get set of all node ids.\n4. Determine root id (parent null).\n5. Create set of required_symbols.\n6. Create set of retained node ids initially all ids.\n7. Create list removal_order empty.\n8. Create list of nodes sorted by depth descending, then by id ascending (stable tie break).\n9. Iterate over sorted nodes:\n   - If node id is root: skip.\n   - If node symbol in required_symbols: skip. (required symbols nodes are never removed even if removable? The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So yes, required symbol nodes are never removed.)\n   - If not removable: skip.\n   - If node has any children that are still in retained set: skip (node with retained children).\n   - Tentatively remove node: check if after removal, all required symbols still present in retained set (excluding this node and its descendants). Actually when we remove a node, we remove its entire subtree. But since we process depth first, we have already removed some of its descendants? Actually if we process deeper first, and we remove a child, that child's descendants are gone. But when we later consider the parent, its children set includes only those that are still retained. So the subtree under the parent includes only those children that remain. So removing the parent would remove those children as well. So we need to check the required symbols among the retained set after removal of the node and its retained descendants. Basically we need to simulate removal of the node and all nodes in its subtree that are still retained. But easier: after removal, the required symbols must still be present in the tree. Check if there exists at least one node for each required symbol among the retained nodes that are not in the subtree of the node being removed. So we need to know the subtree of the node (including itself and all descendants that are still retained). Since we process depth-first and remove only when all children are gone, the subtree under the node may be empty (if all descendants removed). So we can compute the set of node IDs that would be removed if we remove this node: its retained descendants plus itself. Then compute retained after removal = current retained - that set. Then check that for each required symbol, there is at least one node with that symbol in the new retained set. If yes, then remove: update retained set, add node id to removal_order, continue.\n\n10. After processing all nodes, compute sorted retained IDs (list of ids sorted ascending).\n11. Return {\"retained_ids\": sorted_retained_ids, \"removal_order\": removal_order}\n\nEdge cases: if a node has children that are not removable but are required symbols? Then we cannot remove those children, and so the parent has retained children, so parent cannot be removed. That's fine.\n\nThe provided fixtures have simple trees. We need to implement exactly.\n\nAlso note: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That's the check we do.\n\n\"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So sort by depth descending, then id ascending (or any stable? The problem says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" That implies when depths are equal, order by node ID (presumably string compare? IDs are strings like \"n1\", \"n2\", etc. Lexicographic order? Usually IDs are alphanumeric, but we can use natural ordering? Better to use the id string as given, as Python string comparison is stable. But to be safe, we can sort by (depth, id) where depth is negated for descending. But note that depths are integers, so -depth then id.\n\nReturn exactly as specified: sorted retained IDs and actual removal order.\n\nImplementation details: We'll parse case[\"payload\"] which has \"nodes\" list and \"required_symbols\". The function returns a dict.\n\nWe'll raise ValueError for infeasible? Possibly if root not present? But assume valid.\n\nNow produce JSON output with the implementation key containing the Python source.\n\nWe need to escape the source string. Use json.dumps to create the escaped string.\n\nThe response must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". No extra text.\n\nSo final output: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"}\n\nWe'll write the code.\n\nLet's write the solve function:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case[\"payload\"]\n    nodes = payload[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\"required_symbols\"])\n    # Build maps\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n[\"parent\"]\n        if pid is None:\n            root_id = n[\"id\"]\n        else:\n            children[pid].append(n[\"id\"])\n    # Initialize retained set\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    removal_order = []\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n[\"id\"]\n        # Skip root, required symbol nodes\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        if n[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        if not n[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        # Check if node has any retained children\n        # Children that are still in retained set\n        if any(child in retained for child in children[nid]):\n            continue\n        # Compute subtree of retained nodes under this node (including itself)\n        # BFS/DFS to collect all retained descendants\n        subtree = set()\n        stack = [nid]\n        while stack:\n            current = stack.pop()\n            if current in retained:\n                subtree.add(current)\n                for child in children.get(current, []):\n                    stack.append(child)\n        # Now check required symbols after removal\n        # Remove subtree from retained temporarily\n        hypothetical_retained = retained - subtree\n        # Check each required symbol\n        required_ok = True\n        for sym in required_symbols:\n            # Check if any node in hypothetical_retained has this symbol\n            if not any(node_map[nid2][\"symbol\"] == sym for nid2 in hypothetical_retained):\n                required_ok = False\n                break\n        if required_ok:\n            # Perform removal\n            retained = hypothetical_retained\n            removal_order.append(nid)\n    # Sort retained IDs\n    sorted_retained = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"retained_ids\": sorted_retained, \"removal_order\": removal_order}\n\nTest with provided cases mentally? But we just need to output.\n\nNote: The \"removal_order\" should be the order in which nodes were actually removed, i.e., the sequence of IDs. We added them in the order we processed, which is depth descending. That's fine.\n\nPotential issue: When we check children, we used children[nid] list. But children list includes all children (even those removed). We check if any child is in retained. That's correct because we only consider retained children.\n\nBut in subtree calculation, we need to collect only retained descendants. We used a stack that iterates children regardless of retained status; but we only add to subtree if current is in retained. However we also push children even if current is not retained? Actually we only start from nid which is in retained (since it hasn't been removed yet). And we only push children if current is in retained? We push children regardless, but then when we pop child, we check if child in retained. But children of a retained node may not be retained (if they were removed earlier). So we need to only traverse children that are retained, because non-retained children are already gone. Actually if a child was removed earlier, it won't be in retained set. So we should only traverse children that are in retained. So we can check: for child in children.get(current, []): if child in retained: stack.append(child). So modify subtree collection: start with nid in retained, then for each child that is retained, recurse. So subtree includes only retained nodes in its descendant lineage.\n\nAlso, note that we are using children list built from original nodes. That's fine.\n\nEdge: root may have children that are removed. That's okay.\n\nNow, we need to ensure we don't mutate case. We are not.\n\nReturn JSON-serializable.\n\nNow produce final JSON. Use json.dumps to escape.\n\nBut we must output only the JSON object with key implementation. So like:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"}\n\nMake sure no trailing text.\n\nI'll write the code in a string and then use json.dumps to encode it. But since I need to output raw text, I'll manually escape newlines and quotes. Better to use Python's json module to generate the full object, but I cannot run Python here. I'll manually escape.\n\nLet's write the function with proper indentation. Use 4 spaces.\n\nThe code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case[\"payload\"]\n    nodes = payload[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\"required_symbols\"])\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n[\"parent\"]\n        if pid is None:\n            root_id = n[\"id\"]\n        else:\n            children[pid].append(n[\"id\"])\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    removal_order = []\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n[\"id\"]\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        if n[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        if not n[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        if any(child in retained for child in children[nid]):\n            continue\n        # collect retained subtree\n        subtree = set()\n        stack = [nid]\n        while stack:\n            cur = stack.pop()\n            if cur in retained:\n                subtree.add(cur)\n                for ch i